## {{ entry.version }}{% if entry.date %} ({{ entry.date }}){% endif %}

{% if entry.version == "v0.1.0" %}
### Release status

v0.1.0 is cut before the comparator gates (maintainer decision 2026-11-10). It has **not** cleared the three gates of org golden rule 6 (a fidelity sheet from its source, the per-PR delivery smoke, and an anchor reproducing the source's own trend). The anchor is pending. No result from this release counts as a benchmark number, and it does not enter the benchmarks suite until a release clears the gates; that gated release is proposed as v0.2.0 (swarm-routing/.github ROADMAP "Releases"). It ships `core/` (MIT OR GPL-2.0-only, with `core/LICENSE`), the ns-3 contrib module `ara` (`ns3/`, GPL-2.0-only) and `docs/`, as they stand on `main` at the release commit.

{% elif entry.version == "v0.2.0" %}
### Release status

v0.2.0 is the gated release, and its anchor **misses**. Of the three gates of org golden rule 6 it clears two: the fidelity sheet against its source, Güneş, Sorges & Bouazizi, IWAHN 2002 (`docs/fidelity.md`), and the per-PR delivery smoke. The third, the anchor on that paper's trend against AODV, misses: ARA's routing load is below AODV's at every pause time, but its delivery is below AODV's at pause 0 and 30 s, so both gated PDR checks fail (`docs/benchmarks/v0.2.0-anchor.md`; maintainer decision 2026-11-13 on #5). Org rule 6 counts a comparator's numbers only after its three gates, so v0.2.0 ships with that written infeasibility verdict, and its delivery numbers are not presented as a reproduction of the paper. The next anchor is the 2003 version's, a different source with its own trend (#27, #125, v0.3.0). It ships `core/` (MIT OR GPL-2.0-only, with `core/LICENSE`), the ns-3 contrib module `ara` (`ns3/`, GPL-2.0-only) and `docs/`, as they stand on `main` at the release commit; the anchor's raw cells are in the repository at the tag, under `results/cells/`.

